3. Bulk API 2.0:
To avoid transaction limits (CPU timeout, heap size, 50,000 SOQL row limits), move heavy workloads to asynchronous frameworks:
Batch Apex with Database.QueryLocator:
Using a QueryLocator raises the standard 50,000-row SOQL query limit to up to 50 million records.
Keep the batch scope small (e.g., Database.executeBatch(new MyBatch(), 200) or even lower if triggers/flows execute complex logic on each chunk) 
to prevent CPU timeouts and heap overflow.

Bulk API 2.0 for Data Loads:: When loading or extracting millions of records externally, use Bulk API 2.0, 
which handles automatic file chunking and operates asynchronously against the background processing queue.

Database & Storage Architecture:
1. Use skinny Tables(Salesforce need to enable): For heavily queried tables with millions of rows, contact Salesforce Support to create a Skinny Table.
It combines standard and custom fields into a single underlying database view, eliminating the runtime table join between Account and Account_CustomFields.
2. Move cold/historical data out of transactional SObjects.
Use Salesforce Big Objects (capable of holding billions of records, queried via SOQL with defined index keys) for historical audit trails.
Offload data to external data lakes (e.g., AWS S3, Snowflake) and surface it in real time via Salesforce Connect / 
External Objects (OData) without consuming platform storage.

Managing Apex Runtime Limits During Large Runs
1. Disable / Bypass Unnecessary Automation:During large data updates or batches, disable non-essential triggers, flows, and duplicate rules using a configuration bypass pattern
2. Heap Management in Batches:Avoid storing class-level collections that accumulate across batch iterations. Use Database.Stateful strictly for tracking aggregates/counters.

